### Decorator Pattern: Composition Over Inheritance

Decorator wraps an object behind the same interface it implements, adding behavior around calls to the wrapped instance without touching its source. Inheritance bakes every combination of behavior into a fixed class hierarchy at compile time — supporting logging, caching, and validation as independent toggles would need 2³ = 8 subclasses. Decorator makes each concern a separate wrapper that composes at runtime: `new CachingDecorator(new LoggingDecorator(client))`.

```typescript
interface ApiClient {
  request(url: string): Promise<unknown>;
}

class BaseClient implements ApiClient {
  async request(url: string) { return fetch(url).then(r => r.json()); }
}

abstract class ApiClientDecorator implements ApiClient {
  constructor(protected wrapped: ApiClient) {}
  abstract request(url: string): Promise<unknown>;
}

class LoggingDecorator extends ApiClientDecorator {
  async request(url: string) {
    console.log(`-> ${url}`);
    const result = await this.wrapped.request(url);
    console.log(`<- ${url}`);
    return result;
  }
}

class CachingDecorator extends ApiClientDecorator {
  private cache = new Map<string, unknown>();
  async request(url: string) {
    if (this.cache.has(url)) return this.cache.get(url);
    const result = await this.wrapped.request(url);
    this.cache.set(url, result);
    return result;
  }
}

const client = new CachingDecorator(new LoggingDecorator(new BaseClient()));
```

The abstract `ApiClientDecorator` base class matters: it forces every decorator to implement the exact same `ApiClient` interface as what it wraps, so any decorator can wrap any other decorator or the base client interchangeably, in any order, and the whole stack is still assignable wherever an `ApiClient` is expected. If a decorator only implements *some* of the wrapped interface's methods (say it forgets to forward a `cancel()` method that `BaseClient` has), calling code that only knows about `ApiClient` won't notice at compile time — but code that upcasts back to a concrete type, or relies on a method the interface doesn't declare, breaks the moment that decorator sits between it and the base object.

**Common pitfall:** writing a decorator that partially implements the wrapped interface, or forwards only the methods the author remembered to think about. Every decorator must forward the *entire* interface unless deliberately intercepting a method — an incomplete decorator works fine in isolation but silently drops functionality the instant it's composed into a stack with other decorators or consumed through the shared interface type.

