### Chain of Responsibility + Decorator: Two-Directional Middleware

Naive Express-style middleware only intercepts the request on the way *in* — each handler runs before the next, and once `next()` is called, control moves forward and there's no clean way for an earlier handler to run code *after* a later handler (and the actual response) completes, short of wrapping `res.send` itself. Combining Chain of Responsibility (the linked traversal) with Decorator (each link wraps the *call to the next link*, not just the request) gives every handler a natural "before" and "after" phase around the rest of the chain.

```typescript
interface Context { req: unknown; res: unknown; }
type Next = () => Promise<void>;

interface Middleware {
  execute(ctx: Context, next: Next): Promise<void>;
}

class LoggingMiddleware implements Middleware {
  async execute(ctx: Context, next: Next) {
    const start = Date.now();
    await next(); // pre-processing happens above this line, post-processing below
    console.log(`Request took ${Date.now() - start}ms`);
  }
}

class TransactionMiddleware implements Middleware {
  async execute(ctx: Context, next: Next) {
    console.log("BEGIN transaction");
    try {
      await next();
      console.log("COMMIT transaction");
    } catch (err) {
      console.log("ROLLBACK transaction");
      throw err;
    }
  }
}

class MiddlewareChain {
  private handlers: Middleware[] = [];
  use(handler: Middleware) { this.handlers.push(handler); return this; }

  async run(ctx: Context) {
    const dispatch = async (index: number): Promise<void> => {
      if (index >= this.handlers.length) return;
      await this.handlers[index].execute(ctx, () => dispatch(index + 1));
    };
    await dispatch(0);
  }
}
```

This is the onion/middleware-composition model (the same shape Koa uses): each middleware's `await next()` call is the exact point where control passes forward, and everything after that line in the same function runs *after* every downstream handler (and the actual route handler) has finished — including catching errors that bubble back up through `next()`, which is what lets `TransactionMiddleware` commit or roll back based on whether anything deeper in the chain threw.

The real problem this solves that naive stacking can't: cross-cutting concerns that need to wrap the *entire remaining execution*, not just run once before it. Naive middleware (`app.use(logger); app.use(auth); app.use(handler)`) can only ever run code before the next step; it has no way to measure total request duration, wrap the whole downstream chain in a transaction, or catch and react to errors thrown by handlers registered later — those all require code both before and after `next()`, which is exactly the Decorator half of this design (each middleware decorates the *continuation*, not just the initial request).

**Common pitfall:** confusing "the middleware runs before the route handler" with "pre/post hooks," when true pre/post support means code on both sides of the same `next()` call — a middleware chain that only ever calls `next()` as its last statement is CoR without the Decorator half, and can't express transaction boundaries, timing, or downstream error handling no matter how many handlers you stack.

